"{\"artifact_sha256\":\"62978d74b2a68003b79a5f2eea42a766301e869f2a0d763ba982a45faf1255c3\",\"reviewer\":\"/root/luna_history_review_eval/independent_script_reader\",\"review_method\":\"Fresh independent collaboration session read the English narration without the source notes or author verdict on first pass; then read exact revised wording and provided targeted final review.\",\"final_read_findings\":\"Would continue listening. Clarified that company blamed sabotage and the 1925 court-appointed auditor report assigned liability. Retain that distinction. Final sentence returns naturally to the opening wave. Initial concern about attributing the legal conclusion directly to the auditor was clarified by identifying it as a report.\",\"loop\":{\"opening\":\"On a winter day in 1919, a wave of molasses tore through Boston. But the real shock came after the flood.\",\"ending\":\"So when you picture that Boston wave, remember what broke first: not just steel, but the assumption that somebody had checked it.\",\"joined_excerpt\":\"So when you picture that Boston wave, remember what broke first: not just steel, but the assumption that somebody had checked it.\\nOn a winter day in 1919, a wave of molasses tore through Boston. But the real shock came after the flood.\",\"assessment\":\"Natural return from the final image of the wave to the opening wave; asks a resolved accountability question rather than opening an unrelated mystery.\"}}"